WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:14.900
Git automation often uses credentials: SSH keys, deploy keys, access tokens, service accounts, machine users, CI/CD tokens, package registry credentials, or platform-issued identities.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.560
These credentials determine what a script can do.

3
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:25.660
They may allow a clone, a push, a tag creation, a package publication, a release action, or a deployment trigger.

4
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.080
Treat them as operational authority, not as invisible plumbing.

5
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.480
Least privilege starts by matching the credential to the job.

6
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.180
A read-only clone job should not receive write access.

7
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.880
A documentation update job should not have release authority.

8
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.580
A validation workflow should not receive deployment credentials.

9
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.200
A development workflow should not receive production access.

10
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.660
Separate read, write, release, and deployment capabilities where the platform supports it.

11
00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.260
Credentials should be scoped by repository, branch, environment, action, and job.

12
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:07.640
A token that can write to every repository is harder to review and more dangerous than a token scoped to one automation purpose.

13
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:15.260
A deploy key tied to one repository and workflow is easier to rotate than a shared key used across many jobs.

14
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.480
Dedicated identities also improve attribution, monitoring, and incident response.

15
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:29.560
Good key and token management includes ownership, purpose, storage, runtime delivery, rotation, revocation, and access review.

16
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:38.460
Secrets should come from approved secret mechanisms or controlled runtime injection rather than hard-coded files or broad repository variables without scope.

17
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.240
Logs should never expose raw credentials, sensitive headers, private keys, or token values.

18
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:51.140
When a credential is suspected of exposure, teams should be able to revoke or rotate it without guessing where it is used.
